WEBVTT

00:00.450 --> 00:02.971
How can one computer behave like many?

00:02.971 --> 00:07.085
The answer is virtualisation, and the idea
is sixty years old.

00:08.635 --> 00:09.843
Here's how it works.

00:09.843 --> 00:15.824
A thin layer of software, called a
hypervisor, sits between the hardware and
the operating systems.

00:17.274 --> 00:20.943
It splits one physical machine into
several virtual machines.

00:20.943 --> 00:25.274
Each one acts like a complete computer,
while sharing the same hardware.

00:27.024 --> 00:28.997
It began in the 1960s.

00:28.997 --> 00:36.261
Mainframes were huge and expensive, so
IBM's CP-67 let many users share just one.

00:38.611 --> 00:42.609
In the seventies and eighties, cheap
personal computers arrived.

00:42.609 --> 00:46.483
Everyone had their own machine, and
virtualisation went quiet.

00:48.233 --> 00:54.910
In the late nineties, VMware brought it
back, running several operating systems on
one ordinary server.

00:56.660 --> 01:00.920
By the 2000s, data centres were full of
half-idle servers.

01:00.920 --> 01:06.281
Virtualisation consolidated them: fewer
machines, less power, less space.

01:08.031 --> 01:12.225
And in the 2010s, it became the foundation
of the cloud.

01:12.225 --> 01:15.370
AWS, Azure and Google Cloud all run on it.

01:17.120 --> 01:19.851
Quick check. What does a hypervisor
actually do?

01:22.601 --> 01:25.950
It shares one physical machine between
many virtual ones.

01:27.700 --> 01:30.921
One machine. Many computers. That's
virtualisation.
